92186045417 false] [17592186054510 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+10} & =x-2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{x+10})}^{2} & ={(x-2)}^{2} \\\\ x+10 & ={x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x-6 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-1$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-1+10} & \\overset{?}{=}-1-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}-3 \\\\ 3 & \\ne -3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=6$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6+10} & \\overset{?}{=}6-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=6$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{x+10} = x-2$  is $x=6.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054563 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186054563 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054563 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2x - 6} &= 2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{2x - 6})^{2} &= 2^{2} \\\\\n2x - 6 &= 4 \\\\\n2x &= 10 \\\\\nx &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2(5) - 6} &= ^(?)2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &= ^(?)2 \\\\\n2 &= 2✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2\n$$\n\nis $x=5$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186054563 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2x-6} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2x-6})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2x-6 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(5)-6} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2x-6} = 2$  is $x=5.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054564 1071 "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\n1. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n2. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n3. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186054564 1071 "Which of the following equations have no real solution?\n\nI. $\\sqrt{5-x} = -2$\n\nII. $\\sqrt{4-x} = 5$\n\nIII. $\\sqrt{2x+1} = -1$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054565 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186054565 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054565 1074 "First, we isolate the square root by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8} - 2 &= 0 \\\\\n\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x^{2} - 5x + 8})^{2} &= (2)^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x + 8 &= 4 \\\\\nx^{2} - 5x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 4x - x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 4)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\n&= x = 4, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=1$, we get\n$\\sqrt{1^{2} - 5(1) + 8} - 2|= ^(?)0; \\sqrt{4}|= ^(?)2; 2|= 2.✓$\n- For $x=4$, we get\n$\\sqrt{4^{2} - 5(4) + 8} - 2|= ^(?)0; \\sqrt{4}|= ^(?)2; 2|= 2.✓$\n\nBoth $x=1$ and $x=4$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0\n$$\n\nare $x=1$ and $x=4$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186054565 1074 "First, we isolate the square root by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-5x+8})}^{2} & ={(2)}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+8 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x-x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-4)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x=4,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}-5(1)+8}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}-5(4)+8}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=1$ and $x=4$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{x^2-5x+8} -2 = 0$ are $x=1$ and $x=4.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532623 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532644 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532653 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186054873 1050 1080.0 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055250 1071 "Solve for $x$ if given that $\\sqrt{14 - x} = x - 2$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186055250 1071 "Solve for $x$ if given that $\\sqrt{14-x}=x-2.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055250 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{14-x}=x-2$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14-x} & =x-2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{14-x})^{2} & =(x-2)^{2} \\\\ 14-x & ={x}^{2}-4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x+4+x-14 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x+2x-10 & =0 \\\\ x(x-5)+2(x-5) & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =5,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=5$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14-5} & \\overset{?}{=}5-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}5-2 \\\\ 3 & \\overset{?}{=}5-2 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=5$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=-2$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14-(-2)} & \\overset{?}{=}-2-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}-2-2 \\\\ 4 & \\ne -4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-2$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{14-x}=x-2$  is $x=5.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055253 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{1 - 7x} =-3$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186055253 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{1-7x}=-3.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055253 1074 "Remember that a square root is always positive or zero. So, a radical equation has no real solution if it states that a square root is equal to a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the given equation has no real solution." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055470 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{x^{2} - x + 3} - 3 = 0$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186055470 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3}-3=0.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055470 1074 "First, we isolate the square root by adding $3$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3}-3 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3} & =3 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3})}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+3 & =9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-(-2)+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+2+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{9}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 3-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}-3+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{9-3+3}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{9}-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 3-3 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=3$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x+3}-3=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=3.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055941 1064 "When we want to solve a square root radical equation which has the square root on the left-hand side and a linear function on the right-hand side, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2$. This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{2x - 1})^{2} &= (x)^{2} \\\\\n2x - 1 &= x^{2} \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\nHere, we check that the obtained value $x=1$ is actually a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{2(1) - 1} &= ^(?)(1) \\\\\n\\sqrt{1} &= 1^(?) \\\\\n1 &= 1✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x=1$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186055941 1064 "When we want to solve a square root radical equation which has the square root on the left-hand side and a linear function on the right-hand side, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x,\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2.$ This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{2x-1})}^{2} & ={(x)}^{2} \\\\ 2x-1 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\nHere, we check that the obtained value $x=1$ is actually a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(1)-1} & \\overset{?}{=}(1) \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=1} \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{2x-1}=x$ has the solution $x=1.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055984 1064 "To solve a radical equation where the radical is isolated on one side of the equation, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} = 3\n$$\n\nwe square both sides. Remember that squaring is the opposite of the square root, so\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2 =x+1\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{x + 1})^{2} &= 3^{2} \\\\\nx + 1 &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the equation in the standard way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + 1 &= 9 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{(8) + 1} &= ^(?)3 \\\\\n\\sqrt{9} &= ^(?)3 \\\\\n3 &= 3✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1}=3\n$$\n\nis $x=8$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186055984 1064 "To solve a radical equation where the radical is isolated on one side of the equation, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} = 3,\n$$\n\nwe square both sides. Remember that squaring is the opposite of the square root, so $\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2 =x+1,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the equation in the standard way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =9 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8)+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{x+1}=3$ is $x=8.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186055985 1064 "Some radical equations have no solutions. For example, consider the radical equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x} = -2\n$$\n\nThe square root of a number is always positive or zero. So in the equation above, the right-hand side is negative, while the left-hand side is always positive or zero. Consequently the two sides will never be equal, and there is no real solution.\n\nWe could also determine that there is no solution by using the same process as usual. If we try to solve this equation by squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3 - x} &= -2 \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3 - x})^{2} &= (-2)^{2} \\\\\n3 - x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, when we plug this result into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{3 - (-1)} &= ^(?) - 2 \\\\\n\\sqrt{4} &= ^(?) - 2 \\\\\n&= 2 \\mid \\ne - 2\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we *cannot* accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x}=-2\n$$\n\nhas no real solution." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186055985 1064 "Some radical equations have no solutions. For example, consider the radical equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x} = -2.\n$$\n\nThe square root of a number is always positive or zero. So in the equation above, the right-hand side is negative, while the left-hand side is always positive or zero. Consequently the two sides will never be equal, and there is no real solution.\n\nWe could also determine that there is no solution by using the same process as usual. If we try to solve this equation by squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-x} & =-2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3-x})}^{2} & =(-2)^{2} \\\\ 3-x & =4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, when we plug this result into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-(-1)} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we *cannot* accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{3-x}=-2$ has no real solution." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186061188 1078 #edb/ref 17592186061193 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064209 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532723 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064210 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532643 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532634 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532714 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532636 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532666 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064289 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532684 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186064493 1071