ligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{-x^{2}} &= 1 + (-x^{2}) + \\frac{1}{2!}(-x^{2})^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 - x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\inte^{-x^{2}}dx &= \\int(1 - x^{2} + \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} + ⋯)dx \\\\\n&= C + x - \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + \\frac{1}{10}x^{5} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186087639 1074 "The series expansion of $e^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & =1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =1+(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186087640 1071 "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\ln(1+x)$, find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_0^{1/2}\\ln(1-x^2)\\text{d}x}.\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186087640 1071 "Using the standard form of the Maclaurin series of $\\ln(1+x)$, find the first three non-zero terms of the series expansion of the definite integral\n$\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2}\\ln(1-x^2)\\text{d}x}$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186087640 1074 "The power series expansion of $\\ln(1+x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2}) & =(-{x}^{2})-\\frac{1}{2}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(-{x}^{2})^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (-{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{6}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{7}}{7}+⋯){|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {2}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5\\cdot {2}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7\\cdot {2}^{7}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}-\\frac{1}{320}-\\frac{1}{2688}+⋯\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can get a numerical approximation by evaluating the first three terms. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & ≈-\\frac{1}{24}-\\frac{1}{320}-\\frac{1}{2688}=-0.04516\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to five decimal places.\n\nThe exact value of the integral, rounded to five decimal places, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =-0.04523\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our estimation was pretty good!" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186087640 1074 "The power series expansion of $\\ln(1+x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\ln (1 + x) = x - \\frac{1}{2}x^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}x^{3} + ⋯\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-x^2$ in the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - x^{2}) &= (-x^{2}) - \\frac{1}{2}(-x^{2})^{2} + \\frac{1}{3}(-x^{2})^{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&=-x^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{6} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1/2}\\ln (1 - x^{2})dx &= \\int(-x^{2} - \\frac{1}{2}x^{4} - \\frac{1}{3}x^{6} + ⋯)dx \\\\\n&= \\left.(-\\frac{x^{3}}{3} - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{x^{5}}{5} - \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{x^{7}}{7} + ⋯)\\right|_{0}^{1/2} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 2^{3}} - \\frac{1}{2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2^{5}} - \\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 2^{7}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{24} - \\