nt x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186087999 1074 "The series expansion of ${e}^{x}$ is\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-{x}^{2}$ in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =1+(-{x}^{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}(-{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{1}{3!}(-{x}^{2})^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the series by $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}} & =x\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+⋯) \\\\  & =x-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, integrating with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x-{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{5}+⋯)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =C+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{6}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186088853 1064 "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\cos \\sqrt{x} \\, \\textrm dx\\,\n$$\n\nUnfortunately, we cannot compute this integral using any of our standard methods. However, we *can* express this integral as an infinite series if we first write the integrand $\\cos \\sqrt{x}$ as a Maclaurin series.\n\nLet's start with the Maclaurin series expansion of $\\cos{x},$ valid for all $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\cos{x}\t=1 - \\frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \\frac{1}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\,.\n$$\n\n    \n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above equation, we find the series expansion for $\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x} & =1-\\frac{1}{2!}(\\sqrt{x})^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}(\\sqrt{x})^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nIntegrating both sides of this equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\sqrt{x}\\,dx & =\\int (1-\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+⋯)\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{24}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =C+x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{72}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nAnd we're done! We've just found the Maclaurin series expression for $\\displaystyle \\int\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.$\n\nWe can apply the same method to any function $f(x)$ having a power series expansion of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=a_0+a_1 x+a_2 x^2 + \\cdots\\,.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({a}_{0}+{a}_{1}x+{a}_{2}{x}^{2}+⋯)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={a}_{0}\\int \\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{1}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+{a}_{2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+⋯ \\\\  & =C+{a}_{0}x+{a}_{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{a}_{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is valid for the same radius of convergence as the series expansion of $f(x).$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186088853 1064 "Consider the following integral:\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\cos \\sqrt{x} \\, \\textrm dx\\,$\n\nUnfortunately, we cannot compute this integral using any of our standard methods. However, we *can* express this integral as an infinite series if we first write the integrand $\\cos \\sqrt{x}$ as a Maclaurin series.\n\nLet's start with the Maclaurin series expansion of $\\cos{x}$, valid for all $x$:\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} =1 - \\frac{1}{2!}x^2 + \\frac{1}{4!}x^4+\\cdots \\,\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\sqrt{x}$ in the above equation, we find the series expansion for $\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos \\sqrt{x} &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2!}(\\sqrt{x})^{2} + \\frac{1}{4!}(\\sqrt{x})^{4} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{x^{2}}{24} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of this equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\cos \\sqrt{x}dx &= \\int(1 - \\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{x^{2}}{24} + ⋯)dx \\\\\n&= \\int1dx - \\frac{1}{2}\\intxdx + \\frac{1}{24}\\intx^{2}dx + ⋯ \\\\\n&= C + x - \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{1}{24} \\cdot \\frac{x^{3}}{3} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= C + x - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{x^{3}}{72} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done! We've just found the Maclaurin series expression for $\\displaystyle \\int\\cos{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nWe can apply the same method to any function $f(x)$ having a power series expansion of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=a_0+a_1 x+a_2 x^2 + \\cdots\\,\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intf(x)dx &= \\int(a_{0} + a_{1}x + a_{2}x^{2} + ⋯)dx \\\\\n&= a_{0}\\intdx + a_{1}\\intxdx + a_{2}\\intx^{2}dx + ⋯ \\\\\n&= C + a_{0}x + a_{1}\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + a_{2}\\frac{x^{3}}{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is valid for the same radius of convergence as the series expansion of $f(x)$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186088965 1064 "We need to recognize when a particular series looks like a standard Maclaurin series. A list of some common Maclaurin series is given below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{x}^{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-⋯+(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯+\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{ln}(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1] \\\\  & \\operatorname{sin}(x)=x-\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5!}{x}^{5}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{cos}(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will focus on recognizing the standard Maclaurin series for the geometric series, the exponential function, and the natural logarithm" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186088965 1064 "We need to recognize when a particular series looks like a standard Maclaurin series. A list of some common Maclaurin series is given below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{x}^{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-⋯+(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯+\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1] \\\\  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=x-\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5!}{x}^{5}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will focus on recognizing the standard Maclaurin series for the geometric series, the exponential function, and the natural logarithm" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186124450 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532690 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126441 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126442 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126443 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126444 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186126445 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531963 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532013 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532385 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532432 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186126440 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532433 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186126440 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532013 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186127963 1078 #edb/ref 17592186127964 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186128970 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt{x}$?\n\n1. $f(x)$ is always positive.\n2. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty)$.\n3. $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186128970 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) =\\sqrt{x}?$\n\nI. $f(x)$ is always positive.\n\nII. The range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty,0)\\cup (0,\\infty).$\n\nIII. $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186128970 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x\n$$\n\n![](images/f8/f8825f71f4d3565b28198b3a4de5424693adfead87a509f38ffecfbccb9fcd01.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(0)=0 \\not\\gt 0$.\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)$ is $[0,\\infty)$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186128970 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt x.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8825f71f4d3565b28198b3a4de5424693adfead87a509f38ffecfbccb9fcd01.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(0)=0 \\not\\gt 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false since the range of $f(x)$ is $[0,\\infty).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x) \\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x \\rightarrow \\infty.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186128971 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt x{:}\n$$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty)$.\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty)$.\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\nOf the given options, only graph II has all of the above properties.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is only II." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186128971 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt x{:}$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph.\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large.\n\nOf the given options, only graph II has all of the above properties.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is only II." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129152 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532637 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532635 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532654 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129318 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532670 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532658 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532700 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129320 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532703 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129376 1050 420.0 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129490 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 3t - t^{3}, y = 3t^{2}, t ∈ [0, 1]$.\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186129490 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t-{t}^{3},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129490 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-{t}^{3})=3-3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})=6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(3-3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{(6t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9-18{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, factoring the expression $9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9,$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+18{t}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\sqrt{9({t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot |{t}^{2}+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}({t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =18\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3})-(\\frac{0}{5}+\\frac{0}{3})] \\\\  & =18\\pi \\cdot \\frac{8}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{48\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129747 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x) = \\sqrt{x}$?\n\n1. The point $(1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$\n2. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x ∈ [0, ∞)$\n3. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second quadrant" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186129747 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}?$\n\nI. The point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$\n\nII. The domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\nIII. The graph of $f(x)$ lies in the second quadrant" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186129747 1074 "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8825f71f4d3565b28198b3a4de5424693adfead87a509f38ffecfbccb9fcd01.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(1)=\\sqrt{1}=1\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is defined for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186130369 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we create a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0)$.\n- The domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty)$.\n- The range is $y\\in [0,\\infty)$.\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty$, we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty$, though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186130369 1064 "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ First, we create a table of values.\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nWe do not consider negative values for the input because the square root of a negative number is not a real number.\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/39/39c60b7e997c4af7b823796c0b94144572e7bff1be083564ab9fa29fd66d4a42.png)\n\nSome key points to note:\n\n- The graph passes through the points $(1,1)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n- The domain  is $x\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The range  is $y\\in [0,\\infty).$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x \\rightarrow \\infty,$ we have $y\\rightarrow \\infty,$ though the function increases more slowly as $x$ gets large." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186133840 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532694 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186133873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532639 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186133873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532688 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186133898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532677 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186134005 1050 1080.0 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532656 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532628 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186166828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532716 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186166829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532711 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532673 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186166830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532675 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186172090 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532715 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186172135 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532620 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186173258 1064 "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=a \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=b\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186173258 1064 "We can find global extrema of multivariable functions subject to multiple constraints using the method of Lagrange multipliers. \n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = 4xy+z^2\n$$\n\nsubject to the following *two* constraints: \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2=1, \\qquad h(x,y,z)=2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0\n$$\n\nGeometrically, this means that we want to minimize $f(x,y,z)$ over the intersection of the cylinder $x^2+y^2=1$ and the elliptic paraboloid $2x^2 + 4y^2 - z = 0,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b16f73e034724a9f38d91a606f627db85299507552c45192e91c39bc0c6725a.png)\n\nThe method of Lagrange multipliers states that the extrema of the function $f(x,y,z)$ subject to constraints $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b$ can be found by solving the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=a \\\\ h(x,y,z)=b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the constants $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$ are the Lagrange multipliers of this system.\n\nAs before, this method assumes that $\\nabla g \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\nabla h \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ on the curves $g(x,y,z)=a$ and $h(x,y,z)=b,$ and that the extreme values exist.\n\n**Note:** Geometrically, the condition $\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g + \\mu \\nabla h$ states that\n\n- $\\nabla f$ lies in the plane spanned by $\\nabla g$ and $\\nabla h,$ or, equivalently,\n\n- $\\nabla f$ is perpendicular to the level surfaces $g(x,y,z) = a$ and $h(x,y,z) = b.$\n\nWe'll explore this geometric intuition further at the end of the lesson." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186173362 1075 #edb/ref 17592186173363 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186173362 1075 #edb/ref 175