}f.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204570 1074 "Using the result \n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)=2w,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(10‖w{‖}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}^{2})+{∇}_{w}(4) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {∇}_{w}({w}^{T}w)+{∇}_{w}(4) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2w+0 \\\\  & =20w.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204571 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f: R^{3} → R$ defined by $f(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot x$ with respect to $x$ at $x = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186204571 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204571 1074 "Writing $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={b}^{T}x.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of a function $f(x)={b}^{T}x$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204592 1071 "Given the function\n$f(x, w) = 4(‖w‖_{1})^{3} + x^{T}w$\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find $∇_{w}f$. You may assume that $w$ has no zero components." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186204592 1071 "Given the function \n\n$$\nf(x,w)=4{(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3}+{x}^{T}w\n$$\n\nthat maps a vector to a scalar, find ${∇}_{w}f.$ You may assume that $w$ has no zero components." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186204592 1074 "Using the results\n\n$$\n{∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})=\\text{sign}(w),\\,{∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w)=x,\n$$\n\n we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}f & ={∇}_{w}(4{(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3}+{x}^{T}w) \\\\  & ={∇}_{w}(4{(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3})+{∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {∇}_{w}({(‖w{‖}_{1})}^{3})+{∇}_{w}({x}^{T}w) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot {∇}_{w}(‖w{‖}_{1})+x \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)+x \\\\  & =12(‖w{‖}_{1})^{2}\\cdot \\text{sign}(w)+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186205178 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar-valued functions.\n\nRecall that, for a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ to a scalar value $f(\\mathbf{x})$, the *gradient* of $f$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is defined as the column vector of partial derivatives:\n\n![](images/4b/4ba97eb3ffac5de1b569716f0ccaa648749248b62e4918c641f93de84b52b61d.svg)\n\n**Note:** Some resources use $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\mathbf x}$ to denote the gradient. We shall avoid this notation.\n\nRecall that the *total derivative* of a scalar function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the row vector\n\n![](images/9b/9b02d1184f009b66095b1bfd9be6d4d0f29aeb41a0ba08469b404ffc8ca6e88d.svg)\n\nHence, the gradient is simply the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nWe'll now discuss some important results related to gradients widely used in machine learning applications.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186205178 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar-valued functions.\n\nRecall that, for a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ that maps a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ to a scalar value $f(\\mathbf{x})$, the *gradient* of $f$ with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ is defined as the column vector of partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note:** Some resources use\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\mathbf x}\n$$\n\nto denote the gradient. We shall avoid this notation.\n\nRecall that the *total derivative* of a scalar function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with respect to a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is simply the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nWe'll now discuss some important results related to gradients widely used in machine learning applications.\n\nLet's see an example." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186205179 1064 "Let’s derive a general rule for the gradient of a linear function.\n\nSuppose $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, and the scalar function $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{b}^T \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a fixed vector.\n\nWe wish to find an expression for the gradient of $f$.\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{b}^T \\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's write our vectors $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf x$ explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}b_{1} \\\\ b_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ b_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, $\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x$ is the scalar function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{T}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}b_{1} & b_{2} & … & b_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x =b_1x_1+ b_2x_2 + \\cdots + b_nx_n\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x$ with respect to $x_i$ is $b_i$, since all the other terms will vanish.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x_i}\\left(\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf x\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x_i}\\left(b_1x_1+ b_2x_2 + \\cdots + b_i x_i + \\cdots + b_nx_n\\right) = b_i\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x\\right) = \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThis is the multivariable analog of the familiar rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dx}(a x) = a\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186205179 1064 "Let’s derive a general rule for the gradient of a linear function.\n\nSuppose $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and the scalar function $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{b}^T \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a fixed vector. \n\nWe wish to find an expression for the gradient of $f.$\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{b}^T \\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's write our vectors $\\mathbf b$ and $\\mathbf x$ explicitly:\n\n![](images/8a/8a824abda10928a55f4efd94f7c0e4bff0dabf13fc44b789ed53e829971c3471.svg)\n\nThen, $\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x$ is the scalar function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{T}x & =[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & … & {b}_{n}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x\n=b_1x_1+ b_2x_2 + \\cdots + b_nx_n.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x$ with respect to $x_i$ is $b_i,$ since all the other terms will vanish. \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x_i}\\left(\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf x\\right) = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x_i}\\left(b_1x_1+ b_2x_2 + \\cdots + b_i x_i + \\cdots + b_nx_n\\right) = b_i\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all $1\\leq i\\leq n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf b^T \\mathbf x\\right) = \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThis is the multivariable analog of the familiar rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dx}(a x) = a\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186205194 1064 "Consider the $\\textrm{L}_1$ (Manhattan) norm of a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, defined as\n\n$$\n\\\\begin{vmatrix}\\left.\\mathbf{x}\\\\right|_1 = \\sum_{i=1}^n & x_i\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of this function with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$. To do that, let’s focus on finding the partial derivative with respect to a single component $x_k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂‖x‖_{1}}{∂x_{k}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{k}}∑_(i = 1)^(n) \\mid x_{i} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{k}}(\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1} & + & x_{2} & + ⋯ + & x_{k} & + ⋯ + & x_{n}\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂\\begin{vmatrix}x_{1} &}{∂x_{k}} + \\frac{∂\\begin{vmatrix}x_{2} &}{∂x_{k}} + ⋯ + \\frac{∂ \\mid x_{k} \\mid}{∂x_{k}} + ⋯ + \\frac{∂\\end{vmatrix}x_{n}\\end{vmatrix}}{∂x_{k}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{∂ \\mid x_{k} \\mid}{∂x_{k}} \\\\\n&= sign(x_{k})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{sign}(x_k)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{-1,\\begin{vmatrix}x_{k} > 0, \\\\ -1,\\end{vmatrix}x_{k} < 0\n$$\n\nSo, the full gradient of the $\\textrm{L}_1$ norm is simply\n\n$$\n\\left.\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} \\|\\mathbf{x}\\\\right|_1 = \\text{sign}(\\mathbf{x})\n$$\n\nwhich is a vector whose $k$th component is $\\text{sign}(x_k)$.\n\nThe gradient of\n\n$$\n\\left.\\|\\mathbf x\\\\right|_1\n$$\n\nis not defined at points where any component of $\\mathbf x$ equals zero. This is because the absolute value function has a sharp corner at zero and is therefore not differentiable there. Various methods are available to overcome this difficulty when the derivative of the $\\textrm L1$ norm is used in practice, and we'll discuss these in future lessons." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186205194 1064 "Consider the $\\textrm{L}_1$ (Manhattan) norm of a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$, defined as\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf{x}\\|_1 = \\sum_{i=1}^n |x_i|.\n$$\n\nWe want to compute the gradient of this function with respect to $\\mathbf{x}$. To do that, let’s focus on finding the partial derivative with respect to a single component $x_k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂‖x{‖}_{1}}{∂{x}_{k}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}|{x}_{i}| \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}(|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+⋯+|{x}_{k}|+⋯+|{x}_{n}|) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂|{x}_{1}|}{∂{x}_{k}}+\\frac{∂|{x}_{2}|}{∂{x}_{k}}+⋯+\\frac{∂|{x}_{k}|}{∂{x}_{k}}+⋯+\\frac{∂|{x}_{n}|}{∂{x}_{k}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂|{x}_{k}|}{∂{x}_{k}} \\\\  & =\\text{sign}({x}_{k}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{sign}(x_k)$ is defined as\n\n![](images/88/88b4650c41c9ab38ea82851481ed720821ad7f93bfeac56a93d70effe680f230.svg)\n\nSo, the full gradient of the $\\textrm{L}_1$ norm is simply\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} \\|\\mathbf{x}\\|_1 = \\text{sign}(\\mathbf{x}),\n$$\n\nwhich is a vector whose $k$th component is $\\text{sign}(x_k)$. \n\nThe gradient of $\\|\\mathbf x\\|_1$ is not defined at points where any component of $\\mathbf x$ equals zero. This is because the absolute value function has a sharp corner at zero and is therefore not differentiable there. Various methods are available to overcome this difficulty when the derivative of the $\\textrm L1$ norm is used in practice, and we'll discuss these in future lessons." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186205200 1064 "We have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w} \\left(\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\\right) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$\n\nThis result is analogous to the following result from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d w}(w^2) = 2w\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\mathbf w \\in\\mathbb R^n$ be given by\n![](images/ab/abacc87863d62c768803174901464f57748f1488c3518a25cefc06a411ab31f9.svg)\n\nThen, $\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w$ is the *scalar* function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}^{T}w & =[\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} & {w}_{2} & … & {w}_{n}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {w}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\n=w_1^2+ w_2^2 + \\cdots + w_n^2.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w$ with respect to $w_i$ is $2w_i,$ since all the other terms will vanish. Since this is true for all $1\\leq i\\leq n,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w} \\left(\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\\right) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nFinally, note that since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w = ||\\mathbf w ||^2\n$$\n\nwe have the following equivalent result:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}(||\\mathbf w ||^2) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186205200 1064 "We have the following result:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w} \\left(\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\\right) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$\n\nThis result is analogous to the following result from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d w}(w^2) = 2w\n$$\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\mathbf w \\in\\mathbb R^n$ be given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ w_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThen, $\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w$ is the *scalar* function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nw^{T}w &= \\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} & w_{2} & … & w_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}w_{1} \\\\ w_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ w_{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w =w_1^2+ w_2^2 + \\cdots + w_n^2\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w$ with respect to $w_i$ is $2w_i$, since all the other terms will vanish. Since this is true for all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf w} \\left(\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w\\right) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nFinally, note that since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf w^T \\mathbf w = \\left. \\mid \\mid \\mathbf w \\mid \\right|^2\n$$\n\nwe have the following equivalent result:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}(\\mid \\mid \\mathbf w \\mid \\right|^2) = 2\\mathbf w\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186205219 1064 "In many machine learning models, a scalar function depends on a vector that itself depends on another vector.\n\nSuppose $f:\\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\mathbf g:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m$, and define\n\n$$\nh(\\mathbf x)=f(\\mathbf g(\\mathbf x))\n$$\n\nThen the **chain rule for gradients** states:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} h(\\mathbf x) = \\left.J_{\\mathbf g}(\\mathbf x)^T \\,\\nabla_{\\mathbf u} f(\\mathbf u)\\Big\\right|_{\\mathbf u=\\mathbf g(\\mathbf x)}\n$$\n\nwhere $J_{\\mathbf g}(\\mathbf x)$ is the Jacobian of $\\mathbf g$.\n\nIn other words, we have\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Gradient of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{composition}}\\bigg) = \\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Jacobian of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{the inner function}}\\bigg)^T \\cdot \\:\\: \\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Gradient of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{the outer function}}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nLet’s see this in action." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186205219 1064 "In many machine learning models, a scalar function depends on a vector that itself depends on another vector.\n\nSuppose $f:\\mathbb{R}^m \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\mathbf g:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^m,$ and define\n\n$$\nh(\\mathbf x)=f(\\mathbf g(\\mathbf x)).\n$$\n\nThen the **chain rule for gradients** states:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} h(\\mathbf x)\n=\nJ_{\\mathbf g}(\\mathbf x)^T\n\\,\\nabla_{\\mathbf u} f(\\mathbf u)\\Big|_{\\mathbf u=\\mathbf g(\\mathbf x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $J_{\\mathbf g}(\\mathbf x)$ is the Jacobian of $\\mathbf g.$\n\nIn other words, we have  \n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Gradient of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{composition}}\\bigg)   \n= \\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Jacobian of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{the inner function}}\\bigg)^T \n\\cdot \\:\\:\n\\bigg(\\substack{\\Large \\text{Gradient of} \\[3pt] \\Large\\text{the outer function}}\\bigg).\n$$\n\nLet’s see this in action." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186205310 1075 #edb/ref 17592186205311 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186205310 1075 #edb/ref 17592186205312 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] 