efined by\n![](images/9e/9e4b8dccd90c15d35317eb418a99dde9c43f12b8b2780e35292ecc291c10e92d.svg)\nwith respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at  ![](images/37/37e7e5c54e3e7990b627649376f8f55a27db8a82b42b2375f99941793c92ff26.svg)" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208289 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}$ defined by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwith respect to $\\mathbf{x}$ at $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186208289 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n![](images/71/7135a764f77153e7753bd3ab87305f8347207f12d87533565c0018588e00a00f.svg)\nwe have $f(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = (A^T + A)\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n![](images/e5/e5e95b87bd905379b447b17e1ba8946579b85b6ab29831912c154591d5d7bde4.svg)\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at ![](images/5b/5b3d05513f25be9f2d0cc61ba21dbf3b34916f6c2fce1babc8fb4de1627171da.svg)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0 \\\\ 0-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208289 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{x}) = (A^T + A)\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n