d{bmatrix}$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186208924 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\n with respect to $x$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208924 1074 "Writing $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}Ax.\n$$\n\nFor a symmetric matrix $A$, the gradient of the function $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax$ with respect to $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2Ax.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $A$ is symmetric, we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=2[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208929 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208929 1071 "Consider the function $L: R^{3} → R$ defined by $L(x) = 2(Ax - b)^{T}(Ax - b)$ where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is $∇_{x}L(x)$ at $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186208929 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208929 1074 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 32 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208957 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f: R^{2} → R$ defined by\n$f(x) = x^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x$\nwith respect to $x$ at $x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}^{T}$.\n\n| $∇_{x}f =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186208957 1071 "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $x={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}.$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186208957 1074 "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0 \\\\ 3+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186209877 1064 "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$.\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n} = \\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity\n\n$$\n(AB)^T = B^T A^T\n$$\n\n, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{x}f &= (\\frac{df}{dx})^{T} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{df}{dy} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx})^{T} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{T}_(⏟)_(n \\cdot q) \\cdot (\\frac{df}{dy})^{T}_(⏟)_(q \\cdot 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T \\cdot \\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186209877 1064 "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={{{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot {{{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186209878 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar functions to include quadratic functions, quadratic forms, and the chain rule.\n\nConsider the (scalar) **quadratic function**\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. We wish to calculate the gradient of this function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by showing that $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is the same as $\\mathbf x^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf x$, let's first write\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute $\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$ by working step by step.\n\nFirst, we compute the product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$, which gives us an $n\\times 1$ column vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx &= \\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} & ⋯ & a_{1n} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22} & ⋯ & a_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ a_{n1} & a_{n2} & ⋯ & a_{nn}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}a_{11}x_{1} + a_{12}x_{2} + ⋯ + a_{1n}x_{n} \\\\ a_{21}x_{1} + a_{22}x_{2} + ⋯ + a_{2n}x_{n} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ a_{n1}x_{1} + a_{n2}x_{2} + ⋯ + a_{nn}x_{n}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}∑_(j = 1)^(n)a_{1j}x_{j} \\\\ ∑_(j = 1)^(n)a_{2j}x_{j} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ ∑_(j = 1)^(n)a_{nj}x_{j}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $\\mathbf{x}^T$ by this result:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2} & ⋯ & x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nEach term of this product looks like\n\n$$\nx_i \\cdot \\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j\n$$\n\n, so the full expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\left(\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nSo, we've shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.}\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how this helps us to compute the gradient of $f$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186209878 1064 "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar functions to include quadratic functions, quadratic forms, and the chain rule.\n\nConsider the (scalar) **quadratic function**\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. We wish to calculate the gradient of this function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by showing that $f$ can be written in the expanded form \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is the same as $\\mathbf x^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf x,$ let's first write\n![](images/39/394b4241f76616fae493bcc281cbe782c1d1a89680ef24cd2776c02302b9550d.svg)\n\nNow, we compute $\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$ by working step by step.\n\nFirst, we compute the product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$, which gives us an $n\\times 1$ column vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & ⋯ & {a}_{1n} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & {a}_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1} & {a}_{n2} & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}{x}_{1}+{a}_{12}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{1n}{x}_{n} \\\\ {a}_{21}{x}_{1}+{a}_{22}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{2n}{x}_{n} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {a}_{n1}{x}_{1}+{a}_{n2}{x}_{2}+⋯+{a}_{nn}{x}_{n}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{1j}{x}_{j} \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{2j}{x}_{j} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{nj}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $\\mathbf{x}^T$ by this result:\n![](images/15/15180b9898708fa3f2398a534c0e4f0a57f4466abd046b388eb7af3009e534b0.svg)\n\nEach term of this product looks like $x_i \\cdot \\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j$, so the full expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\left( \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nSo, we've shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.}\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how this helps us to compute the gradient of $f.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186209954 1064 "We have the quadratic function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. As we've seen, $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nWe'll use this form to compute the gradient of $f$.\n\nTo compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f$, we'll differentiate this expanded form with respect to $x_k$. First, we express our expanded function as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\sum_{i \\neq k} \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{ij} x_i x_j + \\sum_{i \\neq k} A_{ik} x_i x_k + \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{kj} x_k x_j + A_{kk} x_k^2\n$$\n\nThe idea here is to break up the double summation by isolating all the terms that involve the variable $x_k$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first summation is *independent* of $x_k$,\n- the second and third summations are *linear* in $x_k$, and\n- the final term is *quadratic* in $x_k$.\n\nWe can now compute our derivative term by term in a structured way. Differentiating with respect to $x_k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{k}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{k}}[∑_(i \\ne k)∑_(j \\ne k)A_{ij}x_{i}x_{j} + ∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i}x_{k} + ∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{k}x_{j} + A_{kk}x_{k}^{2}] \\\\\n&= ∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i} + ∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{j} + 2A_{kk}x_{k} \\\\\n&= (∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i}) + A_{kk}x_{k} + (∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{j}) + A_{kk}x_{k} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)A_{ik}x_{i} + ∑_(j = 1)^(n)A_{kj}x_{j} \\\\\n&= (A^{T}x)_{k} + (Ax)_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all\n\n$$\n1\\leq k \\leq n\n$$\n\n, we get the result\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = A^T \\mathbf{x} + A \\mathbf{x} = (A^T + A)\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have the following special cases:\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^T = A$, $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a quadratic form, and our result simplifies to\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = 2 A \\mathbf{x}$.\n- Suppose that $A = I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix. Then, our result for symmetric matrices simplifies to\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{x}) = 2 \\mathbf{x}$\nwhich is a result we saw in a previous lesson." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186209954 1064 "We have the quadratic function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. As we've seen, $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j.\n$$\n\nWe'll use this form to compute the gradient of $f.$\n\nTo compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f$, we'll differentiate this expanded form with respect to $x_k.$ First, we express our expanded function as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) =  \\sum_{i \\neq k} \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{ij} x_i x_j \n+ \\sum_{i \\neq k} A_{ik} x_i x_k \n+ \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{kj} x_k x_j \n+ A_{kk} x_k^2.\n$$\n\nThe idea here is to break up the double summation by isolating all the terms that involve the variable $x_k$. \n\nNotice that\n\n- the first summation is *independent* of $x_k,$\n\n- the second and third summations are *linear* in $x_k,$ and\n\n- the final term is *quadratic* in $x_k.$\n\nWe can now compute our derivative term by term in a structured way. Differentiating with respect to $x_k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{k}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{k}}[{\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{ij}{x}_{i}{x}_{j}+{\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}{x}_{k}+{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{k}{x}_{j}+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k}^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j}+2{A}_{kk}{x}_{k} \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i\\ne k}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i})+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k}+({\\sum }_{j\\ne k}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j})+{A}_{kk}{x}_{k} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{A}_{ik}{x}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{A}_{kj}{x}_{j} \\\\  & =({A}^{T}x)_{k}+(Ax)_{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all $1\\leq k \\leq n$, we get the result\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = A^T \\mathbf{x} + A \\mathbf{x} = (A^T + A)\\mathbf x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have the following special cases:\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^T = A,$ $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a quadratic form, and our result simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = 2 A \\mathbf{x}.\n$$\n\n- Suppose that $A = I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix. Then, our result for symmetric matrices simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T  \\mathbf{x}) = 2 \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhich is a result we saw in a previous lesson." 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216959 1071 "The parabola $y = x^2 + 2$ and the line $y = 3x$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$, where $x_1 < x_2$. What is the value of $x_1 + x_2$?" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216959 1071 "The parabola $y = x^2 + 2$ and the line $y = 3x$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2),$ where $x_1 <        x_2.$ What is the value of $x_1 + x_2?$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216959 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216959 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n{y = x^{2} + 2; y = 3x\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2 &= 3x \\\\\nx^{2} - 3x + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 1)(x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 1, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3\n$$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216960 1071 "The parabola ![](images/49/49d58a04c172fb017d3e63107af8686694a70f8b3b281b61f1bf80fbbce1903c.svg) and the line ![](images/da/dad6e7d0144b11544800cab8e15352011d03b14a429fd6b38518e6d764777fbb.svg) intersect at the points ![](images/58/586d275bc1ac46d094c469d88dfd59d0a63d2ede5496929e15923c42b180fd47.svg) and ![](images/af/afa2f1d8401bc59845387bc9c7d1d6b765abe9c638b3c28f1914f6bd9458e348.svg) where ![](images/ba/bac1330d96cc473aab0c7d4fb929105e307a215a8e6031084e7c9cc61cb87b66.svg) What is the value of ![](images/f5/f52303e047d6e35cf5061a15e4136e4f253bf4cdd2d421145f8a58b05389a469.svg)" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216960 1071 "The parabola $y = -x^2 + x + 4$ and the line $y = 2x + 2$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$, where $x_1 < x_2$. What is the value of $y_1 + y_2$?" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216960 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n{y =-x^{2} + x + 4; y = 2x + 2\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + x + 4 &= 2x + 2 \\\\\n- x^{2} - x + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n- (x^{2} + x - 2) &= 0 \\\\\n- (x + 2)(x - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= -2, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = -2$ and $x_2 = 1$.\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either the curve or the line. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$, we get\n$y|= 2x + 2; = 2(-2) + 2; =-2$,\nso one point of intersection is $(-2,-2)$.\n- Substituting $x=1$, we get\n$y|= 2x + 2; = 2(1) + 2; = 4$,\nso the other point of intersection is $(1,4)$.\n\nThe points of intersection are $(-2,-2)$ and $(1,4)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny_1 = -2,\\; y_2 = 4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny_1 + y_2 = -2 + 4 = 2\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da3cbc81f8170e3cf098a97d249e3ad8f0abf358fa641d8b94b1eb301f1a7858.png)" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216960 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n![](images/31/318d27072067aa70d893f2a1c0dc118ad7caced10b9ca5567b5bb0f91638ef30.svg)\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n![](images/c3/c3bb314de0a4b80fb6d51bbb155484aa968fee78d81011fd578cc8b8bca04df2.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/25/25273d659db203df5af353cdda1a390bdbb635b59363390a0ca00b45fdd24bb2.svg) the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates of the intersection points are ![](images/4f/4fe2b5629a57d8e984a53a0887f0ab10595a82fe303f8c70080cff9039ee200f.svg) and ![](images/42/428ebea63626be22a9e5b18f6f4bd8ceba019bd6d3e685a0621e843604f0f826.svg) \n\nTo find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either the curve or the line. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ![](images/e3/e3f9686f4fa57342e9e4415fe494e0361ef79005a9c92c2192d11f8da980481f.svg) we get\n![](images/6b/6bf49ff9ecea5536117e06be2a55625e2dd056609abec901f615ad74bd52c290.svg)\nso one point of intersection is ![](images/2f/2f7731330530547c0207184c710622d49d8b696419aaba6572ae0b65cc3672ee.svg)\n\n- Substituting ![](images/d7/d7657c785332cdb454794e479e9aae39f6d6f92532f10912f85c1bf3bc2fb317.svg) we get\n![](images/40/406ee9901d6670c3ddb3774fc575c5a1a80d33668dcb345a7a494f297df64147.svg)\nso the other point of intersection is ![](images/f0/f07ed4790b4cd3a20a4af1c48d680f6e6681feb972d35075171975f967eea468.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/1d/1d7e290e5d4bac0018851250cc98d151231495c6cc9187aedaec060d1f9579bf.svg) and\n![](images/a5/a5c25be7330db098773c5566f2ce52d8f6cb65f06e0e9fc9bc4eb0b92a0ba534.svg)\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da3cbc81f8170e3cf098a97d249e3ad8f0abf358fa641d8b94b1eb301f1a7858.png)" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216961 1071 "The parabola $y=-x^2+6x$ and the line $y=9$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q)$. What is $p+q$?" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216961 1071 "The parabola $y=-x^2+6x$ and the line $y=9$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216961 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+6x-9 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+9) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3.$ \n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9).$ Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9,$ and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216961 1074 "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n{y =-x^{2} + 6x; y = 9\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 6x &= 9 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 6x - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n- (x^{2} - 6x + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n- (x - 3)(x - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nx &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3$.\n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9)$. Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9$, and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216962 1071 "Given that the line $y=x +2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=x^2-x+3$, what is the range of possible values of $k$?" 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216962 1071 "Given that the line $y=x +2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186216962 1074 "To determine whether the line\n\n$$\ny=x +2k\n$$\n\nintersects the parabola\n\n$$\ny=x^2-x+3\n$$\n\nwe need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{y = x^{2} - x + 3; y = x + 2k\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - x + 3 &= x + 2k \\\\\nx^{2} - 2x + 3 - 2k &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with\n\n$$\na=1,\\, b=-2\n$$\n\nand $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(3 - 2k)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{4 - 12 + 8k}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{8k - 8}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8k - 8 & < 0 \\\\ 8k & < 8 \\\\ k & < 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt 1$." 13194139535844 17592186045417 false] [17592186216962 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x +2k$ intersects the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-x+3 \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+3 & =x+2k \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+3-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\, b=-2,$ and $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-12+8k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{8k-8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-8 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <8 \\\\ k & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt   1.$" 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186217108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532676 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186217108 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532707 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186217109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532641 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186217109 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532672 13194139535844 17592186045417 true] [17592186217110 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532663 13194139535844 17592186045417 tr